---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { franchises } from '../lib/data.js';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const active = franchises().filter(f => f.status === 'active');
---
<Base title="Home">
  <h1>Ten simulated franchises. One forecast, locked before the auction.</h1>
  <p class="lede">Ten agents, one per current IPL team, bid against each other in a simulated mini-auction on a single open model. Their predictions for the real auction on 16 December 2026 are frozen and hashed the day before, then scored in public against simpler methods.</p>

  <div class="dates" role="list" aria-label="Key dates">
    <div role="listitem"><time datetime="2026-12-15">15 Dec 2026</time><span>Forecast frozen, SHA-256 published</span></div>
    <div role="listitem"><time datetime="2026-12-16">16 Dec 2026</time><span>Real auction</span></div>
    <div role="listitem"><time datetime="2026-12-17">17 Dec 2026</time><span>Scored against four baselines</span></div>
  </div>

  <div class="notice">
    <span class="label">Status</span>
    <p>Under construction. The forecast does not exist yet, so every forecast number on this site is a marked sample until the freeze.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>What it tests</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Does a franchise's own history help?</strong> One shared model with a learned team-identity signal, compared with the same model with that signal switched off. "No" is an acceptable answer.</li>
    <li><strong>A forecast that can't be edited afterwards.</strong> For every player: the chance of being sold, who is likely to buy, and an 80% price range. The file is hashed before bidding starts. <a href={`${base}hash/`}>How to check the hash</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>A public score.</strong> Our model against base price, LightGBM, AuctionIQ and a pundit mock, on the same lots. The <a href={`${base}scorecard/`}>scorecard</a> keeps the rows where we lose.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What it can't tell you</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Backtests on the December 2025 auction are probably contaminated: the base model may have read about it. The 16 December 2026 run is the clean test, which is why the hash matters.</li>
    <li>Seasons have tens to a few hundred lots, so the confidence intervals are wide.</li>
    <li>I couldn't find a published, pre-registered IPL auction forecast that was scored afterwards. That describes my search, not a claim to be first.</li>
    <li>The 2018–21 bid histories come from a source that is withheld.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The ten franchises</h2>
  <ul class="ledger">
    {active.map(f => (
      <li style={`--c:${f.colour}`}>
        <a href={`${base}franchises/${f.id.toLowerCase()}/`}>
          <span class="swatch" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          <span class="name">{f.name}</span>
          <span class="meta">{f.id} · {f.firstSeason}</span>
        </a>
      </li>
    ))}
  </ul>
</Base>
